CAT = "dkmcRoad"; SEG = "ROA"
# Afsnit 5: road design, road surroundings and conditions that change a rider's grip and risk.
Q = [
("In autumn you ride along a road through a beech wood. What surface hazard should you expect?",
 "Wet leaves that cut grip", ["Fresh tar that softens", "Loose sand from beaches", "Ice only on the road bridges"],
 "In autumn, fallen leaves along woods make the road greasy. On two wheels that loss of grip can bring the bike down in a bend or under braking."),
("In spring and autumn, why can country roads near fields be slippery?",
 "Soil left by tractors", ["Pollen from the trees", "Salt from road gritting", "Dust from the verges"],
 "Tractors going to and from field work leave soil on the road in spring and autumn, which makes the surface greasy, especially after rain."),
("When the temperature hovers around freezing, where is patchy ice most likely?",
 "On and under bridges", ["On wide, open straights", "On roads through villages", "In sunny town streets"],
 "In the transition seasons, patchy ice forms where the road crosses damp areas such as woods, bogs and hollows, along earth banks and on and under bridges."),
("Which of these gives a motorcycle the least grip when wet?",
 "A painted road marking", ["Rough new asphalt", "A dry concrete slab", "Coarse new road chippings"],
 "Road markings, metal covers, rails and cobbles all have poor friction and give a motorcycle much less grip, especially in the wet. Stay upright as you cross them."),
("Where on a narrow country road is the risk of accidents usually greatest?",
 "Where small side roads join", ["On long, straight stretches", "Next to wide verges", "Just after a speed sign"],
 "On narrow roads the accident risk is greatest where minor side roads join and at entrances to properties, where vehicles may pull out."),
("Compared with a road with a cycle track, how much higher is the risk of hitting cyclists on a road without one?",
 "About twice as high", ["About the same", "About ten times higher", "Slightly lower"],
 "Roads without cycle tracks, both in the country and in towns, carry about double the risk of hitting cyclists and a higher risk for moped riders."),
("You ride a dark country road with no pavement. Who are you most likely to come upon on the right of the carriageway?",
 "Pedestrians without reflectors", ["Cyclists on the cycle track", "Parked lorries with their lights on", "Children on a school patrol"],
 "Roads without pavements bring a risk of hitting pedestrians on the right of the carriageway, especially in the dark, as many do not wear reflectors."),
("Where is the risk of hitting a crossing pedestrian higher than elsewhere?",
 "Just before and after a crossing", ["On a raised footbridge over the road", "Far from any shops or homes", "Inside a pedestrian tunnel"],
 "The risk of hitting crossing pedestrians is greater in the areas just before and after a pedestrian crossing than on stretches with no crossing at all."),
("Why do lane and edge lines along a road help a rider most in poor light?",
 "They guide your position", ["They mark the speed limit", "They give extra grip", "They warn of speed cameras"],
 "Lines along the road help road users place themselves and manoeuvre correctly, and this help is especially great in poor light."),
("A four-lane road has a central reservation but no crash barrier. Which risk does it reduce?",
 "Head-on collisions", ["Pedestrians crossing", "Single-vehicle crashes", "Cyclists crossing"],
 "A central reservation reduces head-on collisions and stops left turns to and from minor roads, but without a barrier or fence it does little against crossing cyclists and pedestrians."),
("Which kind of road has an increased risk of single-vehicle crashes and head-on collisions?",
 "Narrow roads", ["Motorways", "Pedestrian streets", "Wide four-lane roads"],
 "Narrow roads carry an increased risk of single-vehicle crashes, head-on collisions and hitting moped riders, cyclists and pedestrians."),
("You ride on a well-built road with a high speed limit and light traffic. What should you remember?",
 "Still ride to the conditions", ["Always use the full limit", "Grip is guaranteed there", "You may relax your scanning there"],
 "Be critical of the urge to ride at the maximum on roads of good standard without regard to traffic and conditions. Road safety features do not remove the need for care."),
("In which kind of town street is the accident risk greatest?",
 "Shopping streets", ["Ring roads with no homes", "Industrial estate roads", "Roads beside a park"],
 "In built-up areas the risk is greatest in shopping streets and on residential roads, with many pedestrians, parked vehicles and junctions."),
("A cyclist leaves a cycle track mid-block and swerves onto the carriageway in front of you. Who must give way?",
 "The cyclist", ["You, as the faster vehicle", "Whoever is on the right", "Nobody, it is a lane change"],
 "Cyclists and moped riders who turn from a cycle track onto the carriageway, other than where it ends at a junction, have an unconditional duty to give way, but they do not always do so."),
("Is a wide four-lane road generally much safer than a two-lane road?",
 "No, not significantly", ["Yes, about twice as safe", "Yes, for motorcycles only", "Only after dark"],
 "Wide four-lane roads are in general not significantly safer than two-lane roads. Ride to the conditions on both."),
("Where along a road is a strong side wind most likely to push your motorcycle off line?",
 "On exposed bridges", ["In a sheltered village street", "In a long tunnel", "Between tall hedges"],
 "Strong side wind can knock a motorcycle off course, especially at high speed. Expect gusts on exposed bridges, embankments and gaps between woods; the Side wind sign (A 95) warns of it."),
]
